// The same mistake, in a literal that also holds a real node. Not caught —
// and that is what this file is here to show.
//
//   emo check mixed.emo    # expected: no diagnostic
//
// A literal whose elements share no type degrades to `Array[Unknown]`
// instead of reporting a conflict, and `Unknown` conforms to everything
// (`conforms`, emo_check.ml:1011), recursing into `ArrayType` like it does
// for any other container. So one stray element poisons the whole array's
// type and every element in it goes unchecked. `[text("ok"), "x"]` is
// accepted as `Array[Node]`; `[1, "x"]` is accepted as `Array[Int64]`.
//
// This is the same mechanism that hides the each_from bug in
// screens_a.emo: there the element arrives through a Block, here through a
// disagreeing literal, and in both cases the element type collapses to
// Unknown and the check goes silent. The A spelling does not close the
// hole on its own — only a spelling that types each child slot separately,
// as a parsed tree literal would, does that.

def broken() spine.Node {
  return spine.column([spine.text("ok"), "not a node"])
}
